<Wiki
  title="puv why"
  description="Explain why a package or an extension is installed in this project."
>
  <p class="kicker">Wiki</p>
  <h1>why</h1>
  <p>
    <code>puv why</code> answers why a package or an extension is installed. The argument is a
    package name or an extension name.
  </p>

  <h2>A package you added</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv add symfony/console
puv why symfony/console</pre>
  <p>
    <code>symfony/console</code> is there because <code>[dependencies]</code> names it. That is
    the direct case.
  </p>

  <h2>A package you did not type</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv list
puv why &lt;name-from-that-list&gt;</pre>
  <p>
    <a href="/wiki/commands/list"><code>puv list</code></a> shows dependencies of the packages
    you added. Pass one of those names to <code>why</code> when you want the requirement that
    brought it in, rather than the direct line from
    <a href="/wiki/commands/add"><code>add</code></a>.
  </p>

  <h2>An extension</h2>
  <pre class="block">puv why ext-mbstring</pre>
  <p>
    Extensions are virtual packages. <code>why</code> names the requirement that pulled
    <code>ext-mbstring</code> in. If a required extension is missing from the runtime, the failure
    happens earlier, at <a href="/wiki/commands/lock"><code>lock</code></a> time, and names the
    extension. That rule is <a href="/wiki/resolution">resolution</a>.
  </p>
</Wiki>
